SUMMARY:Forest Germans between Geography\, History and Memory: Mapping a F
 orgotten Settlement Region in Southern Poland — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261119T121500
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261119T133000
DTSTAMP:20261112T071646Z
UID:25a3c91bbb221be57016ba747ede@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Academic\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:One of the most intriguing paradoxes in Central European histo
 ry is that a Polish cultural and territorial community is known as the For
 est Germans\, a name reflecting the complexity of its identity and history
 . The Forest Germans emerged through centuries of cultural and biological 
 synthesis\, combining German influences with West Slavic and\, above all\,
  Polish elements. Their history dates back to the 14th century\, when medi
 eval German newcomers and West Slavic settlers from Germania Slavica\, thr
 ough intermingling with the Polish population\, gradually underwent comple
 te Polonization between the 16th and 18th centuries. Through assimilation
 \, acculturation\, adaptation\, and cultural exchange\, th\nCategory: Acad
 emic\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/forest-germans-betw
 een-geography-history-and-memory-mapping-a-forgotten-settlement-region-in-
 southern-poland
LOCATION:1957 E Street NW\, 1957 E St NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/forest-germans-between-geography-histor
 y-and-memory-mapping-a-forgotten-settlement-region-in-southern-poland
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
